{"content_hash": "d7f3e5f7ab1df322", "version_id": 232, "article_id": 24, "level_code": "en_a2", "lang": "en", "level": 2, "level_label": "A2 初级", "title": "The Plague Is Still Here: Cases Appear Every Year", "title_zh": "鼠疫其实一直没消失，每年都还有人得病", "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-plague-cases-africa-year.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://scx1.b-cdn.net/csz/news/tmb/2026/the-plague-never-went.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 215, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "The plague is a very old disease. Many people think it is gone, but it is not. Every year, some people still get sick.", "lead_zh": "鼠疫是一种很古老的疾病。很多人以为它早就消失了，但其实没有。每年都还有人感染。", "preview": "The plague killed many people in Europe long ago. But it never went away. Today, doctors can treat it with antibiotics. Most sick people liv", "preview_zh": "很久以前，鼠疫在欧洲夺走了很多人的生命。但它从未消失。如今，医生可以用抗生素治疗它。大多数感染者生活在非洲，尤其是刚果和马达加斯加。在美国，每年约有七人感染鼠疫。他们通常住在美国西部的乡村地区。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 221, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@d7bba21/content/audio/24/en_a2.mp3", "duration": 91.638, "size_bytes": 555120, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 250, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@d7bba21/content/data/versions/232.json", "paragraphs": ["The plague killed many people in Europe long ago. But it never went away. Today, doctors can treat it with antibiotics. Most sick people live in Africa, especially in Congo and Madagascar. In the United States, about seven people get the plague each year. They usually live in the rural West.", "The plague is caused by bacteria called Yersinia pestis. These bacteria live in animals like rats and prairie dogs. Fleas bite the animals, then bite people. This spreads the disease. People can also get sick by touching sick or dead animals.", "There are three forms of the plague. Bubonic plague is the most common. It makes the lymph nodes very painful and swollen. Pneumonic plague infects the lungs. Septicemic plague infects the blood. All three are dangerous without early treatment.", "Recently, a worker at a plague research institute in Siberia died. Russian authorities say tests showed no plague. But the institute is now under quarantine. Experts say the plague will not spread widely. It is hard to catch from another person.", "The plague can be cured. The key is to see a doctor fast. Antibiotics work well if you start them early. There is a vaccine, but it is only for people with a high risk. There is no plague vaccine in the U.S."], "paragraphs_zh": ["很久以前，鼠疫在欧洲夺走了很多人的生命。但它从未消失。如今，医生可以用抗生素治疗它。大多数感染者生活在非洲，尤其是刚果和马达加斯加。在美国，每年约有七人感染鼠疫。他们通常住在美国西部的乡村地区。", "鼠疫是由一种叫鼠疫耶尔森菌的细菌引起的。这种细菌生活在老鼠和草原犬鼠等动物身上。跳蚤咬了这些动物，再咬人，就会传播疾病。人们接触生病或死亡的动物也可能被感染。", "鼠疫有三种类型。腺鼠疫最常见，它会让淋巴结非常疼痛和肿胀。肺鼠疫感染肺部。败血型鼠疫感染血液。这三种如果不及时治疗都很危险。", "最近，西伯利亚一家鼠疫研究所的一名工作人员去世了。俄罗斯当局说检测显示没有鼠疫。但该研究所现在被隔离了。专家表示鼠疫不会大规模传播。它很难从一个人传染给另一个人。", "鼠疫是可以治好的。关键是要尽快看医生。如果尽早使用抗生素，效果很好。有一种疫苗，但只给感染风险高的人用。美国没有鼠疫疫苗。"], "body": "The plague killed many people in Europe long ago. But it never went away. Today, doctors can treat it with antibiotics. Most sick people live in Africa, especially in Congo and Madagascar. In the United States, about seven people get the plague each year. They usually live in the rural West.\n\nThe plague is caused by bacteria called Yersinia pestis. These bacteria live in animals like rats and prairie dogs. Fleas bite the animals, then bite people. This spreads the disease. People can also get sick by touching sick or dead animals.\n\nThere are three forms of the plague. Bubonic plague is the most common. It makes the lymph nodes very painful and swollen. Pneumonic plague infects the lungs. Septicemic plague infects the blood. All three are dangerous without early treatment.\n\nRecently, a worker at a plague research institute in Siberia died. Russian authorities say tests showed no plague. But the institute is now under quarantine. Experts say the plague will not spread widely. It is hard to catch from another person.\n\nThe plague can be cured. The key is to see a doctor fast. Antibiotics work well if you start them early. There is a vaccine, but it is only for people with a high risk. There is no plague vaccine in the U.S.", "body_zh": "很久以前，鼠疫在欧洲夺走了很多人的生命。但它从未消失。如今，医生可以用抗生素治疗它。大多数感染者生活在非洲，尤其是刚果和马达加斯加。在美国，每年约有七人感染鼠疫。他们通常住在美国西部的乡村地区。\n\n鼠疫是由一种叫鼠疫耶尔森菌的细菌引起的。这种细菌生活在老鼠和草原犬鼠等动物身上。跳蚤咬了这些动物，再咬人，就会传播疾病。人们接触生病或死亡的动物也可能被感染。\n\n鼠疫有三种类型。腺鼠疫最常见，它会让淋巴结非常疼痛和肿胀。肺鼠疫感染肺部。败血型鼠疫感染血液。这三种如果不及时治疗都很危险。\n\n最近，西伯利亚一家鼠疫研究所的一名工作人员去世了。俄罗斯当局说检测显示没有鼠疫。但该研究所现在被隔离了。专家表示鼠疫不会大规模传播。它很难从一个人传染给另一个人。\n\n鼠疫是可以治好的。关键是要尽快看医生。如果尽早使用抗生素，效果很好。有一种疫苗，但只给感染风险高的人用。美国没有鼠疫疫苗。", "text": "The plague killed many people in Europe long ago. But it never went away. Today, doctors can treat it with antibiotics. Most sick people live in Africa, especially in Congo and Madagascar. In the United States, about seven people get the plague each year. They usually live in the rural West. The plague is caused by bacteria called Yersinia pestis. These bacteria live in animals like rats and prairie dogs. Fleas bite the animals, then bite people. This spreads the disease. People can also get sick by touching sick or dead animals. There are three forms of the plague. Bubonic plague is the most common. It makes the lymph nodes very painful and swollen. Pneumonic plague infects the lungs. Septicemic plague infects the blood. All three are dangerous without early treatment. Recently, a worker at a plague research institute in Siberia died. Russian authorities say tests showed no plague. But the institute is now under quarantine. Experts say the plague will not spread widely. It is hard to catch from another person. The plague can be cured. The key is to see a doctor fast. Antibiotics work well if you start them early. There is a vaccine, but it is only for people with a high risk. There is no plague vaccine in the U.S.", "vocab": [{"word": "plague", "pos": "noun", "zh": "鼠疫", "note": "一种严重的传染病"}, {"word": "antibiotics", "pos": "noun", "zh": "抗生素", "note": "用来杀死细菌的药物"}, {"word": "bacteria", "pos": "noun", "zh": "细菌", "note": "复数形式，单数是 bacterium"}, {"word": "flea", "pos": "noun", "zh": "跳蚤", "note": "一种会咬人的小虫子"}, {"word": "lymph nodes", "pos": "noun", "zh": "淋巴结", "note": "身体里的小豆状器官"}, {"word": "symptom", "pos": "noun", "zh": "症状", "note": "生病时身体的表现"}, {"word": "quarantine", "pos": "noun", "zh": "隔离", "note": "防止疾病传播的措施"}, {"word": "vaccine", "pos": "noun", "zh": "疫苗", "note": "预防疾病的注射剂"}], "title_original": "The plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year", "summary_original": "The plague, the rare bacterial infection infamously known for killing tens of millions in 14th-century Europe, never went away. It is now easily treated with antibiotics.", "body_original": "The plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year\nby The Associated Press\nedited by\nAndrew Zinin\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\nreputable news agency\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nThe plague, the rare bacterial infection infamously known for killing tens of millions in 14th-century Europe, never went away. It is now easily treated with antibiotics.\nSince the 1990s, most human cases have been reported in Africa, especially Congo and Madagascar, according to the\nWorld Health Organization\n.\nThere are an average of about seven human cases per year in the U.S.,\naccording to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\n, mostly in rural areas of the American West.\nA worker at a plague research institute in Siberia recently died of pneumonia.\nThe case has raised alarm\nin Russia after unconfirmed media reports suggested she might have died of pneumonic plague. Russian authorities say testing showed no evidence of plague, but the research institute has been quarantined.\n\"People think the plague is gone, it was something that occurred 500 years ago. It's not. It's here amongst us,\" said Simon Clarke of the University of Reading in the U.K. \"Unless you get rid of those wild animals, which of course is not possible, then you're not going to get rid of the bacteria.\"\nSurprised to hear the plague is still around? Here's what to know.\nPlague can attack lymph nodes, lungs and blood\nPeople in Siberia have been dying of plague for at least 5,500 years. The earliest evidence of a form of the disease was\nfound in teeth from ancient hunter-gatherers\nnorth of the region's Lake Baikal.\nPneumonic plague and the more well-known bubonic plague—also called the Black Death—are caused by the same bacteria, called Yersinia pestis.\nThe bacteria spread naturally among rodents like prairie dogs and rats. Fleas that jump from infected animals and bite people can spread the disease.\nThe bubonic plague is the most common form of the infection. It causes painfully swollen lymph nodes that are most commonly found in the groin, armpit and neck, called buboes. Other symptoms include sudden high fever and chills, headaches, and pain in the abdomen, legs and arms, according to the Cleveland Clinic.\nThere are two other forms of the plague: pneumonic plague, which can be caused by inhaling the bacteria, infects the lungs, while septicemic plague is an infection in the bloodstream. Both are fatal if not treated early, according to the World Health Organization.\nBubonic plague will often develop into the other two forms of plague if untreated.\nPlague can spread through fleas or bodily fluids\nOther than bites from infected fleas, people can also get plague by touching infected bodily fluids, so health experts recommend taking extra care when handling dead or sick animals.\nThe plague can also spread through the respiratory droplets of someone who has pneumonic plague.\n\"If you cough, you can spread it to another person, but that doesn't travel very far,\" said Dr. Glenn Wortmann, MedStar Washington Hospital Center. \"So you really need to be in face-to-face contact with that person to get pneumonic plague.\"\nThat's why \"the chance of this spreading widely in Russia and then extending here to the U.S. will not happen,\" he said.\nAntibiotics can treat plague\nThe plague was never eradicated, but we've gotten better at preventing its spread and treating it in humans.\nWhen treated early with antibiotics, the plague can be cured. The key is to get to a doctor fast—otherwise the plague can be deadly.\nThere is a vaccine, but the WHO only recommends it for people who are at high risk of infection, like laboratory and healthcare workers. There isn't strong evidence that vaccines can work against the pneumonic form.\nThere's no plague vaccine available in the U.S.\nKey medical concepts\nBubonic Plague\nPneumonic Plague\nYersinia pestis\nAntibiotics\nClinical categories\nInfectious diseases\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nWho's behind this story?\nAndrew Zinin\nMaster's in physics with research experience. Long-time science news enthusiast. Plays key role in Science X's editorial success.\nFull profile →\n© 2026 The Associated Press. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten or redistributed without permission.\nCitation\n:\nThe plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-plague-cases-africa-year.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T09:31:25.521123+00:00", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "